WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.940
JOE: There are many charities,
organisations and community

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.310
support groups who work to
support the needs of individuals

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.850
with autism and their families.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.049
They may be involved in purely providing
support, guidance and advice or may be

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:18.439
involved in campaigning for improved
rights, services and opportunities

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.480
like the ‘National Autistic Society’.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.210
BETH: It doesn’t matter how big or small
the organisation or group is, their main

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.709
aim is usually to help to create a better
functioning society for individuals

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.370
with autism and their families.

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:35.440
JOE: Autism charities can offer a
large amount of information on their

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.340
sites, with links to other charities
support agencies and online forums.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.390
Some even have directories giving
lists of services available.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.769
The ‘NHS’ website itself
has a lot of information and

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:49.210
links to other organisations.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.299
BETH: Social media such as ‘twitter’
and ‘facebook’ can help give links

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.120
to information for many different
kinds of support, along with personal

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.890
advice and event information.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.780
Often charities will have their
own facebook page or twitter feed.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.310
JOE: There are many people with experience
of autism offering support and sharing

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.489
their stories on forums and social media.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.690
It’s not always necessary to talk to
others in online groups, but reading or

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:19.210
hearing about other people's experiences,
can help make it easier to understand some

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.429
of the problems, and how they may relate.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.510
BETH: Comments on social media are
often based on personal experience of

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.539
individuals and families and should
be taken in the context in which

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:33.050
they are written, and not as advice
that will help someone directly.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.600
Also, any information on the internet,
unless it’s from a registered or

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.240
official source, should always be
double checked and cross referenced,

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.709
to make sure that it is correct and
supported by the appropriate authorities.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.620
JOE: Local councils can help
with advice and support and

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.440
maybe able to organise financial
benefits, depending on situation.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.450
For children and young people under
25 councils have a ‘local offer’

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:01.180
which is support for young people
with special educational needs.

00:02:01.719 --> 00:02:05.520
For adults with autism or parents
and carers, a council can do a

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.759
‘needs’ assessment or a ‘carers’
assessment to find out what problems

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.500
there are, and if any support or
financial benefits might be available.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.290
BETH: Some people might be wary of
the internet or about letting people

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.940
know their situation, but there
is a lot of information, help and

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.559
support available online or by phone.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.359
Also support doesn’t always have
to be given online, once links are

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.500
established, face to face support

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.040
can develop, if this preferred.

